      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1,\n\\]\n\nwe start by identifying the standard form of the hyperbola equation. The standard form for such a hyperbola is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a^2 = 27\\) and \\(b^2 = 11\\). From this, we can determine \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3},\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\sqrt{11}.\n\\]\n\nFor a hyperbola of the form \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\), the vertices are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). Therefore, the vertices of this hyperbola are at \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\).\n\nThe distance between these two vertices is the distance between the points \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\). This distance is simply the difference in the y-coordinates of these points:\n\n\\[\n3\\sqrt{3} - (-3\\sqrt{3}) = 3\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}.\n\\]",
