      "text": "The given equation of the hyperbola is \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\). This is a standard form of a hyperbola that opens vertically, where the standard form is \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\). By comparing, we can identify \\(a^2 = 27\\) and \\(b^2 = 11\\). Therefore, \\(a = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{11}\\).\n\nThe vertices of a hyperbola that opens vertically are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). So, the vertices are at \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\).\n\nThe distance between the vertices is the distance between these two points, which is \\(2a\\). Therefore, the distance between the vertices is:\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\\).",
